---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import { getEntry } from "astro:content";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Dashboard from "../layouts/Dashboard.astro";
import { route } from "../lib/route";

const summary = await getEntry("analysis", "summary");
const reportContext = await getEntry("reportContext", "context");
const projectName =
	reportContext?.data.title ?? summary?.data.metadata.name ?? "Planetnik";
---

<Dashboard title="About" projectName={projectName}>
  <div class="about">
    <Hero tagline="Read the weather of the machine" />

    <p class="lead">
      <strong>Płanetnik</strong> is a tool for documenting and analysing the architecture of complex software systems.
    </p>

    <p>
      Built in the spirit of pragmatic architectural analytics, <strong>Płanetnik</strong> helps teams understand large
      codebases directly from the source. No long workshops. No endless interviews. Just show the code.
    </p>

    <p>
      Płanetnik extracts structural signals from source repositories and turns them into usable insight about system
      shape, technical debt, coupling, complexity, ownership, drift, and entropy.
    </p>

    <p>
      It also aims to make a harder leadership idea tangible in code: <strong>activity is visible, impact is structural</strong>.
      The Impact page turns that principle into evidence about where engineering work is reshaping the system
      rather than simply adding visible motion.
    </p>

    <p>
      Where other tools stop at raw metrics, Płanetnik aims to reveal the <strong>weather system</strong> of the
      codebase: the pressure fronts, storm zones, fault lines, unstable regions, and slow entropic decay that shape
      software delivery over time.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>What Is Płanetnik?</h3>

    <p>
      In Polish folklore, a <strong><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C5%82anetnik" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Płanetnik</a></strong> is a being associated with clouds, storms, hail, and shifting
      weather.
    </p>

    <p>For this project, that metaphor becomes a way to think about software systems.</p>

    <p>Large codebases have weather:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>pressure builds</li>
      <li>turbulence spreads</li>
      <li>local changes become system-wide storms</li>
      <li>fragile areas attract repeated disruption</li>
      <li>hidden structural debt shapes the forecast</li>
      <li>entropy pulls structure away from its intended form</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Płanetnik</strong> exists to make that weather visible.</p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Why Płanetnik?</h3>

    <p>
      Modern systems are often too large, too entangled, and too fast-moving to understand through documents alone.
    </p>

    <p>
      Płanetnik provides a pragmatic and relatively inexpensive way to examine source code repositories and derive
      meaningful architectural insight from what is actually there, not what people remember, assume, or hope is true.
    </p>

    <p>It helps you answer questions like:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>where is the codebase growing fastest?</li>
      <li>which parts of the system are most coupled?</li>
      <li>where is complexity starting to gather?</li>
      <li>which boundaries are healthy, and which are breaking down?</li>
      <li>what areas are structurally fragile?</li>
      <li>where does technical debt behave like a recurring storm front?</li>
      <li>where is entropy eroding architectural intent?</li>
      <li>how do code structure and team structure interact?</li>
      <li>what hidden forces are driving delivery pain?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="section-banner">
      <Image
        src={route("/plantnik-banner.webp")}
        alt="Planetnik banner"
        width={1536}
        height={1024}
        class="section-banner-image"
      />
    </div>

    <hr />

    <h3>What Płanetnik Helps You See</h3>

    <dl>
      <dt>size</dt>
      <dd>how big different parts of the system are, and where growth is concentrated</dd>

      <dt>complexity</dt>
      <dd>where cognitive load and implementation burden are increasing</dd>

      <dt>coupling</dt>
      <dd>which modules, domains, or services pull too hard on each other</dd>

      <dt>hotspots</dt>
      <dd>where structural risk, churn, and change pressure overlap</dd>

      <dt>ownership</dt>
      <dd>how code and people interactions align or conflict</dd>

      <dt>drift</dt>
      <dd>where the architecture is moving away from its intended shape</dd>

      <dt>entropy</dt>
      <dd>where disorder is accumulating through unmanaged change, weakened boundaries, duplicated logic, and unclear responsibility</dd>

      <dt>turbulence</dt>
      <dd>where the system experiences repeated instability, friction, and operational strain</dd>

      <dt>team topology signals</dt>
      <dd>how delivery structure and software structure influence one another</dd>
    </dl>

    <hr />

    <h3>Approach</h3>

    <blockquote>Do not begin with stories. begin with the code.</blockquote>

    <p>
      Płanetnik is inspired by the mighty <a href="https://www.sokrates.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sokrates</a>, a pragmatic source code examination tool
      created by Željko Obrenović. It borrows from the same philosophy of examining codebases directly, but adds its own
      structure, modelling, and analysis on top.
    </p>

    <p>
      Rather than treating the repository as an opaque blob, Płanetnik helps organise and interpret what is found:
      source patterns, file groups, module boundaries, dependency signals, historical pressure points, ownership clues,
      architectural zones, and areas of structural erosion.
    </p>

    <p>The result is a richer architectural picture built from evidence already present in the repository.</p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Core Ideas</h3>

    <h4>1. Show the Code</h4>
    <p>The fastest path to architectural truth often starts with direct inspection of the repository.</p>

    <h4>2. Software Has Weather</h4>
    <p>
      Technical debt is not always a buried ruin. Sometimes it is atmospheric: diffuse, recurring, mobile, and only
      obvious when the pressure changes.
    </p>

    <h4>3. Entropy Is Always at Work</h4>
    <p>
      Without active design, systems drift. Boundaries soften. Responsibility spreads. Local fixes accumulate. The
      architecture becomes harder to reason about.
    </p>
    <p>Płanetnik helps teams see that entropic movement before it becomes a full storm.</p>

    <h4>4. Structure Matters</h4>
    <p>
      Files, directories, modules, packages, domains, and boundaries all leave traces. Płanetnik helps interpret those
      traces.
    </p>

    <h4>5. Architecture Should Be Observable</h4>
    <p>Architecture should not live only in slide decks and memory. It should be measurable, inspectable, and reviewable.</p>

    <h4>6. Socio-Technical Systems Leave Patterns</h4>
    <p>
      Code shape, change patterns, ownership, and team interaction often reinforce each other. Płanetnik helps surface
      those relationships.
    </p>

    <h4>7. Evidence Beats Ceremony</h4>
    <p>
      Rich architectural insight does not always require expensive process. A great deal can be learned by examining the
      codebase with care, structure, and the right lens.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>The Weather Model</h3>

    <dl>
      <dt>pressure</dt>
      <dd>delivery demand, backlog load, rushed change, and local compromise</dd>

      <dt>turbulence</dt>
      <dd>frequent breakage, unclear boundaries, unstable dependencies, and repeated rework</dd>

      <dt>storm fronts</dt>
      <dd>areas where high change, high coupling, and high complexity converge</dd>

      <dt>drift</dt>
      <dd>the slow movement away from intended architecture, domain boundaries, or operational clarity</dd>

      <dt>entropy</dt>
      <dd>the background tendency of systems to decay into disorder unless actively maintained</dd>

      <dt>visibility</dt>
      <dd>the ability to observe structural reality rather than rely on intuition alone</dd>
    </dl>

    <p>This is not just metaphor. It is a practical lens for understanding why large systems become harder to change.</p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Use Cases</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>document the architecture of a large codebase</li>
      <li>understand a legacy system</li>
      <li>prepare for domain decomposition</li>
      <li>examine monolith boundaries</li>
      <li>identify hotspots and unstable areas</li>
      <li>support modernisation efforts</li>
      <li>validate architectural assumptions</li>
      <li>detect erosion of intended boundaries</li>
      <li>make technical debt easier to discuss in concrete terms</li>
      <li>support engineering leadership with grounded structural insight</li>
      <li>improve conversations around ownership and team design</li>
      <li>detect where entropy is outpacing maintenance</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h3>Who It Is For</h3>

    <ul>
      <li>architects</li>
      <li>staff and principal engineers</li>
      <li>tech leads</li>
      <li>platform teams</li>
      <li>engineering managers</li>
      <li>modernisation teams</li>
      <li>anyone trying to understand what a system is really doing beneath the surface</li>
    </ul>

    <hr />

    <h3>Philosophy</h3>

    <p>Płanetnik is not a replacement for engineering judgement.</p>

    <p>It is a way to ground judgement in evidence.</p>

    <p>
      It does not promise perfect truth. It provides a better forecast. Its goal is not to eliminate uncertainty, but to
      make hidden structure, mounting pressure, and entropic risk more legible.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h3>Design Goals</h3>

    <dl>
      <dt>pragmatic</dt>
      <dd>useful on real repositories without massive setup</dd>

      <dt>fast</dt>
      <dd>designed for iterative analysis, not slow ceremonial reporting</dd>

      <dt>grounded</dt>
      <dd>based on the source itself</dd>

      <dt>polyglot-friendly</dt>
      <dd>applicable across mixed-language codebases</dd>

      <dt>architecture-aware</dt>
      <dd>focused on structural insight, not just code metrics</dd>

      <dt>socio-technical</dt>
      <dd>aware that systems are shaped by both code and people</dd>

      <dt>composable</dt>
      <dd>suitable for pipelines, automation, and repeatable analysis</dd>

      <dt>entropy-conscious</dt>
      <dd>built to help teams spot decay before it hardens into chronic failure</dd>
    </dl>

    <hr />

    <h3>Example Questions</h3>

    <ul class="questions">
      <li>where are storm fronts forming in the codebase?</li>
      <li>which modules attract repeated change pressure?</li>
      <li>where is coupling strongest?</li>
      <li>which areas combine high size, high churn, and high complexity?</li>
      <li>where are boundaries porous or breaking down?</li>
      <li>where is entropy accumulating fastest?</li>
      <li>which teams are exposed to the most architectural turbulence?</li>
      <li>what parts of the system feel calm, but are structurally unstable underneath?</li>
      <li>what is the likely cost of leaving this area untouched for another quarter?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Dashboard>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: none;
  }

  .about > :not(.section-banner) {
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  p {
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  hr {
    border: none;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin: 2rem 0;
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  h4 {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  dl {
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem;
  }

  dt {
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  dd {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  blockquote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    margin: 1rem 0;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    background: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.04);
    border-radius: var(--radius-attached);
  }

  code {
    font-family: "SF Mono", "Fira Code", monospace;
    font-size: 0.85em;
    background: var(--color-surface-muted, #f1f5f9);
    color: var(--color-text, inherit);
    padding: 0.15em 0.4em;
    border-radius: var(--radius-inline);
  }

  .questions li {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .section-banner {
    width: 100%;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.5rem;
  }

  .section-banner-image {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
  }
</style>
